{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>כִּבְרַת אָרֶץ (a stretch of land).</b> Menachem explained it as the expression \"כַּבִּיר\" (great), meaning a great distance. And there is an Aggadah (Bereishit Rabbah 82:7) that it means when the land is hollowed out like a sieve (כִּכְבָרָה), when the lamp is available and the winter has passed but the scorching heat has not yet come. But this is not the plain sense of Scripture, for in the case of Naaman we find (II Kings 5:19) \"and he went from him כִּבְרַת אָרֶץ.\" And I say that it is a noun denoting a land-measurement, as Rashi's language suggests (Rashi on Genesis 35:16). The correct view is what Rabbi David Kimchi thought, that the כ is for comparison and is not radical, and the word appears in \"הָיוּ לְבָרוֹת לָמוֹ\" (Lamentations 4:10), \"וְתַבְרֵנִי לֶחֶם\" (II Samuel 13:5) — the meaning being a small amount of eating in the morning, and its interpretation is the measure of a land-journey from morning until mealtime, for so all wayfarers estimate. This I wrote at first, but now that I have merited and come myself to Jerusalem — praise to the good El who bestows good — I saw with my own eyes that from Rachel's tomb to Bethlehem there is not even a mil, and behold this explanation is refuted, as well as Menachem's words. Rather, it is a noun of land-measurement according to Rashi's words, and it contains no adjective but only an agreed-upon designation, as do most nouns, and the כ is for use (prepositional), meaning it was not measured precisely. And if this name were an adjective, it could be \"בְּרַת\" like \"בַּת,\" from the expression \"מַה בְּרִי וּמַה בַּר בִּטְנִי\" (Proverbs 31:2), and it would be a name for the small measure that wayfarers use, like the mil of today, and it would be called \"בַּת אָרֶץ\" because the measure is like a daughter to the parasah or to another measure known in those days. I also saw that there is no burial site in Ramah nor anything near it; rather, the Ramah belonging to Benjamin is about four parasangs away from it, and the Ramah in the hill country of Ephraim (I Samuel 1:1) is more than two days' journey from it. Therefore I say that the verse which says \"קוֹל בְּרָמָה נִשְׁמָע\" (Jeremiah 31:14) is a metaphor in the manner of a parable, meaning that Rachel was crying out in a loud voice and bitter lamentation, until the sound was heard far away in Ramah, which is on the hilltop toward her son Benjamin, for he is not there and she is bereft of them. Scripture did not say \"בְּרָמָה רָחֵל מְבַכָּה עַל בָּנֶיהָ\" but rather that there the voice was heard. And it appears to me that Jacob buried her on the road and did not bring her into the city of Bethlehem of Judah that is nearby, because he foresaw through the holy spirit that Bethlehem Ephrathah would belong to Judah, and he did not want to bury her except in the territory of her son Benjamin, and the road where the pillar stands is near Bethel, in the territory of Benjamin. And so they said in Sifrei (Zot HaBerachah, Deuteronomy 33:11): she died in the portion of Benjamin, as is found in the parashah of Zot HaBerachah. And I saw that Jonathan ben Uzziel was sensitive to this, for he translated: \"a voice was heard on high in the world\" (קַל בְּרוּם עָלְמָא אִשְׁתְּמַע), and he translated the entire verse as referring to the congregation of Israel."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The verse citation from Bereishit Rabbah is given as chapter 82, section 7 following the standard chapter division. Ramban cites Sifrei on Deuteronomy 33:11 though the reference to Benjamin's portion in connection with Rachel's death is more commonly associated with Sifrei on the verse about Benjamin's blessing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:05.656846381Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}